--TEST--
`Core\Test::expectFailure` requires that its body failed an assertion, reads the ledger rather than the throw, and is itself a failure when the body passed -- `rule:testing/failure-ledger`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The member inverts the usual reading: a body whose assertion *failed* is the
// passing row, and a body that ran clean is the refusal. What it asks is what
// the ledger says, not what class came back out of the body -- which is why
// the third row below, whose body caught its own failure and returned
// normally, is still discharged. That row is § 5's silently-passing test, and
// it is the one a member written over the throw instead of over the ledger
// would let through.
//
// The `callable` is written as an anonymous function at each call site: a block body
// declares its return type, and a method reference `Class::method(...)`
// does not exist yet, so there is no shared body to name.

int $discharged = 0;
int $refused = 0;

// The ordinary row: one failing assertion, discharged, and nothing propagates.
try { Core\Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => { Core\Test::assertTrue(false); }); $discharged = $discharged + 1; } catch (Throwable $plain) { echo $plain->message, "\n"; }

// The inverted row: a body in which nothing failed is itself a failure, and it
// arrives as an ordinary catchable throw like any other assertion's.
try { Core\Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => { Core\Test::assertTrue(true); }); $discharged = $discharged + 1; } catch (Throwable $passing) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $passing->message, "\n"; }

// The ledger, not the throw. This body swallows its own failure and returns
// normally, so nothing about its control flow says a thing went wrong -- and
// it is still discharged, because the failed assertion was recorded when it
// ran and a `catch` does not unrecord it.
try {
    Core\Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => {
        try { Core\Test::assertSame(1, 2); } catch (Throwable $swallowed) { }
    });
    $discharged = $discharged + 1;
} catch (Throwable $caught) { echo $caught->message, "\n"; }

// Only the failures are discharged: a body that passed one assertion and
// failed another is the same accepting row as the first, the passing one
// having really run.
try { Core\Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => { Core\Test::assertTrue(true); Core\Test::assertNull(1); }); $discharged = $discharged + 1; } catch (Throwable $mixedBody) { echo $mixedBody->message, "\n"; }

// Counted, not read off the lines: three accepting bodies with nothing in
// common but a recorded failure, and one refusal. A member reading the throw
// would keep the first and the last and lose the middle one.
echo "discharged=", $discharged, "/3 refused=", $refused, "/1\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Test::expectFailure failed: the callable ran without a failed assertion
discharged=3/3 refused=1/1
